.docname {Architecture}
.include {docs}

gol-bevy is a Bevy app split into plugins around a plain-Rust core. The core (`app`, `rule`, `chunk`, `board`, `sparse_board`, `universe`, `settle`, `patterns`, `cell_image`, `cell_gif`, `workspace`) knows nothing about Bevy's UI or rendering, so it is tested directly.

## Source layout

.filetree
    - src
      - main.rs
      - lib.rs
      - app.rs
      - app
        - history.rs
        - stamp.rs
        - selection.rs
        - playback.rs
      - rule.rs
      - chunk.rs
      - board.rs
      - sparse_board.rs
      - universe.rs
      - settle.rs
      - patterns.rs
      - cell_image.rs
      - cell_gif.rs
      - workspace.rs
      - atomic_file.rs
      - shared.rs
      - keybindings.rs
      - feedback.rs
      - frame_pacing.rs
      - gol_plugin.rs
      - coords.rs
      - settings_plugin.rs
      - ui_input.rs
      - canvas_view.rs
      - render_plugin.rs
      - render_plugin
        - board_texture.rs
        - finite_view.rs
        - preview.rs
        - frame_loop.rs
      - plane_view.rs
      - input_plugin.rs
      - input_plugin
        - shortcuts.rs
        - pointer.rs
        - cursor.rs
        - camera.rs
      - simulation_plugin.rs
      - native_ui_plugin.rs
      - native_ui_plugin
        - layout.rs
        - actions.rs
        - sync.rs
        - accessibility.rs
        - controls.rs
        - dialogs.rs
        - icons.rs
        - minimap.rs
        - palette.rs
        - sparkline.rs
        - speed.rs
        - theme.rs
        - toolbar.rs
    - assets
      - patterns
      - fonts
    - benches
    - build.rs

| Module                  | Responsibility                                                                |
|-------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `app`                   | Application state and user actions: tools, stamps, speed, undo and redo, import and export |
| `rule`                  | Life-like rules, and reading and writing their rulestrings                    |
| `chunk`                 | The 64 × 64 chunks of cells at a bit each that both boards are made of, and the row-at-a-time step and parallel bands both use |
| `board`                 | The finite board: edge modes, and a grid of chunks                            |
| `sparse_board`          | The unbounded board: a map of the same chunks                                 |
| `universe`              | Either kind of board behind signed cell coordinates, and the snapshots undo keeps of them |
| `settle`                | Noticing a board that repeats, from generation fingerprints                   |
| `patterns`              | Built-in patterns and RLE reading and writing                                 |
| `cell_image`            | A rectangle of the board as a bit per cell, and the PNG drawn from it         |
| `cell_gif`              | Recording generations of a copy of the board as a GIF, each frame only what changed |
| `workspace`             | Versioned workspace files, checked completely before they replace anything    |
| `atomic_file`           | Writing through a temporary file, so a failed save keeps the old contents, and waiting briefly for a file held on Windows |
| `shared`                | App-wide Bevy state: the simulation resource, system sets, overlay toggles, themes, board size settings |
| `keybindings`           | Key binding constants, typed characters, and the Help and README controls lists built from them |
| `feedback`              | Short notices shown after edits from the toolbar or the keyboard              |
| `frame_pacing`          | Updating every frame while anything moves, and only on input once nothing does |
| `gol_plugin`            | Everything the app adds to Bevy's default plugins, so `main.rs` and the schedule test build the same app |
| `coords`                | Cells in world space, cell size, zoom and pan limits, and fitting the camera to a board or pattern |
| `settings_plugin`       | Loading and saving `settings.toml`                                            |
| `ui_input`              | Deciding whether the pointer and keyboard belong to the UI or the board       |
| `canvas_view`           | The board camera's viewport, and fitting the board into it                    |
| `render_plugin`         | The world of a finite board: its texture, grid, edge walls, and the stamp preview |
| `plane_view`            | Drawing the unbounded board, on a texture the size of the canvas              |
| `input_plugin`          | Keyboard shortcuts, mouse editing, and camera zoom and pan                    |
| `simulation_plugin`     | Advancing generations on time, requesting redraws, and quitting               |
| `native_ui_plugin`      | Toolbar, palette, status bar, and dialogs, in Bevy UI                         |

`app.rs` holds the `App` struct, its constructor, and the board editing at its core. Each concern adds its own `impl App` block in a module of `app`:

| Module                  | Responsibility                                                                |
|-------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `history`               | Undo and redo, edit strokes, and the board before a run that reset returns to |
| `stamp`                 | The pattern library, pasting, and turning, flipping, and placing the stamp    |
| `selection`             | The Select tool's rectangle, and copying it or the board as RLE or an image   |
| `playback`              | Running, speed, settling and pausing by itself, and the population history    |

`render_plugin` and `input_plugin` keep their plugin wiring in the parent file, and their systems in modules of their own:

| Module                  | Responsibility                                                                |
|-------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `board_texture`         | The board's tiles, the cell ages they show, and painting the cells that changed |
| `finite_view`           | The grid, the edge walls and their pulse, and resizing the board to its settings |
| `preview`               | The stamp under the pointer, the hovered cell, and the selection rectangle    |
| `frame_loop`            | A headless copy of the frame, for the frame-loop benchmark                    |
| `shortcuts`             | Keyboard shortcuts, and finishing a paste                                     |
| `pointer`               | Drawing, erasing, stamping, and selecting with the mouse                      |
| `cursor`                | The keyboard's cell cursor                                                    |
| `camera`                | Zooming, panning, and fitting the view                                        |

`native_ui_plugin` builds the interface and keeps it in step with the app. Its parts are modules of their own:

| Module                  | Responsibility                                                                |
|-------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `layout`                | Spawning the interface, the canvas and panel rectangles, and where the UI takes the pointer |
| `actions`               | What each button does, shared by clicks and assistive technology              |
| `sync`                  | Keeping buttons, labels, and the status bar in step with the app each frame   |
| `toolbar`               | The single toolbar row, and the menu that takes the workspace controls in narrow windows |
| `controls`              | Everyday controls, shortcut hints, and first-use guidance                     |
| `speed`                 | The speed field: scrubbing, direct entry, step buttons, and the ∞ toggle      |
| `palette`               | Pattern search, library groups and their thumbnails, and the stamp preview    |
| `dialogs`               | The import, export, settings, help, and diagnostics dialogs, and their drafts |
| `minimap`               | The map of the whole board shown while part of it is out of view              |
| `sparkline`             | The line of recent population in the status bar                               |
| `icons`                 | Toolbar icons, drawn at startup rather than loaded from assets                |
| `theme`                 | Colour tokens, and recolouring the interface when the theme changes           |
| `accessibility`         | AccessKit names, values, and actions, and keyboard navigation                 |

`lib.rs` exposes every module, so `main.rs`, the benchmarks, and the tests use the same code.

## Plugins

`main.rs` builds the app from Bevy's `DefaultPlugins` (with a 1200 × 800 window and an 800 × 420 minimum) and `GolPlugin` from `gol_plugin.rs`, which adds the shared resources, orders the system sets, and adds the project's plugins:

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        main[main.rs] --> DP[DefaultPlugins]
        main --> GP[GolPlugin]
        GP --> PL["Project plugins<br/>RenderPlugin, PlaneViewPlugin, CanvasViewPlugin, SettingsPlugin,<br/>UiInputPlugin, NativeUiPlugin, InputPlugin, SimulationPlugin, FramePacingPlugin"]
        PL --> S[(Simulation resource)]
        S --> APP[app::App]
        APP --> U[universe::Universe]
        U --> B[board::Board]
        U --> SB[sparse_board::SparseBoard]
        APP --> P[patterns]

All plugins share one `Simulation` resource, which owns the `app::App` and with it the board: a `Universe`, which is either a finite `Board` or the unbounded `SparseBoard`. `RenderPlugin` draws the first and `PlaneViewPlugin` the second. The UI and the keyboard call the same `App` methods, so a button and its shortcut always behave alike, and both go through the same undo history.

`tests/frame_schedule.rs` builds the same app without a window event loop or a GPU and runs it for a few frames. The plugins' systems are tested one at a time in their own modules; this test checks that, in the order `GolPlugin` gives them, a click on the board draws, is redrawn the next frame, and a click on the palette over the board doesn't reach it.

## Frame order

Systems run in ordered sets, so each frame sees a consistent state.

At **startup**:

1. `StartupSet::LoadSettings`: read `settings.toml` into the layout, board size, and grid resources.
2. `StartupSet::BuildWorld`: create the board, sized from settings or else from the window, and the scene.

Then the simulation settings (rule, edges, speed) are applied. They are startup state, not edits, so they don't enter undo history.

Every **frame**, `Update` runs these sets in order:

| Set          | Work                                                                 |
|--------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `Simulation` | Run the generations that are due                                     |
| `Camera`     | Zoom, pan, and fit                                                   |
| `Resize`     | Apply board size changes                                             |
| `Redraw`     | Collect whether anything needs redrawing                             |
| `Render`     | Update the board texture, grid, walls, and preview                   |
| `Quit`       | Exit if asked                                                        |

Keyboard shortcuts and mouse editing run later, in the `Input` set of `PostUpdate`, after Bevy has updated cameras and transforms. That way a click maps to the cell that was under the cursor in the frame it happened.

## Idle frames

Bevy updates and draws every frame by default, which keeps a paused window busy. `FramePacingPlugin` switches Bevy's `WinitSettings` between three modes at the end of each frame:

- **Busy**: every frame, as Bevy's default does, while the board runs at max speed, a notice or a pulse fades, a key or button is held, a text field has focus, a file is being read or written, or Diagnostics is on, and for `SETTLE` (one second) after any of those or any input. Edits made from input are drawn a frame later, and a tooltip, the minimap, and saving settings each follow input after a short delay of their own. Each of those delays checks at compile time that it fits in `SETTLE`.
- **Paced**: while a run at a set speed is all that changes, two updates a generation, and at least ten a second. Two rather than one, so every generation is shown however the frames fall. A profile of a small board running at 40 generations a second put 4% of the busy CPU time in the app's own systems and the rest in Bevy's per-frame work and rendering, so the number of frames is what costs: at 10 generations a second this took the app from about 98% of a CPU core to 22%.
- **Idle**: an update only when input arrives, or every 5 seconds as a backstop (60 seconds while another window has focus).

Anything that changes on screen without input, such as a background task finishing, writes a `StayAwake` message on every frame it is busy. With a screen reader connected the app stays busy, as its requests reach the app without waking it.

## Shared resources

| Resource                            | Holds                                                         |
|-------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------|
| `Simulation`                        | The `App`, the step timer, average step time, measured rate, and the unbounded board's cell at the world origin |
| `BoardSettings`                     | The chosen board size, or that it should fill the canvas      |
| `RedrawRequested`                   | Whether this frame needs a redraw                             |
| `DiagnosticsOverlay`, `GridOverlay` | Whether diagnostics and the grid are on                       |
| `CellAge`                           | Whether cells are shaded by age, with trails                  |
| `UiInputRegions`, `UiInputCapture`  | Where the UI is, and whether it has the pointer or keyboard   |

## Input routing

The UI publishes the rectangles it occupies and whether a dialog is open into `UiInputRegions`. `ui_input` turns that, together with the focused widget, into `UiInputCapture`:

- **Pointer** input over a panel or under a dialog goes to the UI, so clicks on the palette never draw on the board.
- **Keyboard** input goes to the UI while a dialog is open or a text field or slider has focus.

Board shortcuts run only when `canvas_keyboard_available` allows it. This keeps the routing independent of how the UI is drawn.

## Key bindings

Key bindings are `KeyBinding` constants in `keybindings.rs`, each with its keys, label, and action. The same constants drive the keyboard handler, the in-app help text, and the README test, so the documentation of a key cannot drift from what it does.

## Rendering

The board is drawn as 256 × 256-cell tiles, each a texture with one texel per cell, scaled up by the camera. Only cells that changed are rewritten, using the masks of changed cells the board keeps, and only the tiles that hold them are sent to the GPU again. Operations that touch everything, such as resizing, randomizing, and clearing, fall back to a full redraw. See [Simulation engine](engine.qd#rendering) for details.

The unbounded board has no size to make a texture of, so `plane_view` keeps one the size of the canvas and paints whatever part of the plane is in view. See [Unbounded board](engine.qd#unbounded-board).
